{
  "id": "grammar.a1.06.reflexive",
  "title": "Reflexive Verbs",
  "sections": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Reflexive Verbs",
      "content": "Many daily activities in Spanish use reflexive verbs. These describe things you do to yourself. You add 'me' before the verb: levantarse becomes 'me levanto' (I get up), and ducharse becomes 'me ducho' (I shower)."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "title": "The pattern",
      "rows": [
        [
          "levantarse",
          "to get up"
        ],
        [
          "me levanto",
          "I get up"
        ],
        [
          "ducharse",
          "to shower"
        ],
        [
          "me ducho",
          "I shower"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "examples",
      "title": "A Morning Routine",
      "items": [
        {
          "spanish": "Me levanto a las siete.",
          "english": "I get up at seven."
        },
        {
          "spanish": "Me ducho y me visto.",
          "english": "I shower and get dressed."
        },
        {
          "spanish": "Desayuno café con tostadas.",
          "english": "I have coffee and toast for breakfast."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "tip",
      "content": "Not every daily-routine verb is reflexive. 'Desayunar' takes no 'me' — you say 'desayuno', not 'me desayuno'."
    },
    {
